praisal clause","Umpire","Property claim valuation","Hurricane deductibles","Ice dam claims","Rowhouse roofing"],"priceRange":"$$","openingHoursSpecification":[{"@type":"OpeningHoursSpecification","dayOfWeek":["Monday","Tuesday","Wednesday","Thursday","Friday"],"opens":"08:00","closes":"18:00"}]},{"@type":"WebSite","@id":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/#website","url":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/","name":"Maryland Insurance Appraiser","publisher":{"@id":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"inLanguage":"en-US"},{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-glossary/#webpage","url":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-glossary/","name":"Insurance Appraisal Glossary: 40 Claim Terms Defined","description":"Searchable glossary of insurance appraisal and claim terms: ACV, RCV, umpire, appraisal award, O&P, ice dam, MJIA, hurricane deductible and more.","inLanguage":"en-US","isPartOf":{"@id":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/#website"},"about":{"@id":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"dateModified":"2026-11-12","primaryImageOfPage":{"@type":"ImageObject","@id":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-glossary/#primaryimage","url":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/assets/img/photos/calculator.webp","width":960,"height":626},"breadcrumb":{"@id":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-glossary/#breadcrumb"},"mainEntity":{"@id":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-glossary/#definedtermset"}},{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Appraisal glossary","item":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-glossary/"}],"@id":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-glossary/#breadcrumb"},{"@type":"DefinedTermSet","name":"Insurance appraisal glossary","hasDefinedTerm":[{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Actual cash value (ACV)","description":"The value of damaged property after depreciation is subtracted from its replacement cost. Many policies pay ACV first and the rest after repairs are completed."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Additional living expenses (ALE)","description":"Extra costs of living elsewhere while a home is uninhabitable after a covered loss, such as rent and higher food costs. Usually paid under Coverage D."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Appraisal award","description":"The signed written decision that sets the amount of loss. Under most policies it is valid once any two of the three panel members sign it."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Appraisal clause","description":"The policy condition that lets either party resolve a disagreement over the amount of loss through two appraisers and an umpire rather than in court."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Appraisal demand","description":"The written notice one party sends the other to invoke the appraisal clause, usually naming the claim and the party’s appraiser."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Betterment","description":"An improvement beyond the pre-loss condition of the property. Insurers may argue that part of a repair is betterment and not owed."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"COMAR","description":"The Code of Maryland Regulations. Title 31 contains insurance regulations, including standards for how insurers handle claims."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Coverage A – Dwelling","description":"The part of a homeowners policy that covers the house itself and attached structures such as an attached garage or deck."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Coverage B – Other structures","description":"Coverage for detached structures on the property, such as a detached garage, shed or fence, often set as a percentage of Coverage A."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Coverage C – Personal property","description":"Coverage for belongings such as furniture, clothing and electronics, whether at home or, within limits, elsewhere."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Coverage D – Loss of use","description":"Coverage for additional living expenses or lost rental income while the property can’t be used after a covered loss."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Declarations page","description":"The summary page of a policy listing the insured, the property, coverage limits, deductibles and endorsements."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Depreciation","description":"The reduction in value for age, wear and condition. It is subtracted from replacement cost to reach actual cash value."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Derecho","description":"A long-lived, widespread windstorm from a line of thunderstorms. The June 29, 2012 derecho caused heavy tree and roof damage across Maryland."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Disinterested appraiser","description":"An appraiser with no personal or financial stake in the outcome beyond a normal fee. Most appraisal clauses require it."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Examination under oath","description":"A formal, recorded interview of the policyholder under oath that many policies allow the insurer to request during a claim investigation."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Hurricane deductible","description":"A deductible, often a percentage of Coverage A, that applies to damage from a hurricane or named storm as the policy defines it. Common near the Chesapeake Bay and the coast."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Ice dam","description":"A ridge of ice at the roof edge that traps melting snow, which can back up under shingles and into the home. A frequent winter problem in Garrett and Allegany counties."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Line item","description":"A single entry in a repair estimate showing a task or material, its quantity, unit and price."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Maryland Insurance Administration (MIA)","description":"The state agency that regulates insurers in Maryland and takes consumer complaints. It is led by the Maryland Insurance Commissioner."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Maryland Joint Insurance Association (MJIA)","description":"Maryland’s insurer of last resort for property owners who can’t obtain coverage in the regular market, similar to a FAIR plan."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Matching","description":"The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs match in color, texture or style, such as siding or flooring."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Mitigation","description":"Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping a roof or drying out water. Most policies require it."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Mortgagee clause","description":"The policy provision that protects the mortgage lender’s interest, which is why claim checks are often made out to both owner and lender."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Named storm","description":"A storm given a name by the National Hurricane Center. Some policies use this term to trigger a special percentage deductible."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Nor’easter","description":"A coastal storm with strong northeast winds, heavy rain or snow and tidal flooding. The 1962 Ash Wednesday storm is a well-known Maryland example."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Ordinance or law coverage","description":"Coverage for added costs of meeting current building codes during repairs, such as upgraded electrical work or roof decking. Often limited unless increased by endorsement."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Overhead and profit (O&P)","description":"A general contractor’s charge for running the job and earning a margin, often shown as 10% overhead and 10% profit in estimates. A frequent point of dispute."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Party wall","description":"A shared wall between two attached buildings, typical of Baltimore rowhouses. Fire, water and roof damage can cross party walls and complicate claims."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Proof of loss","description":"A sworn statement of the amount claimed that some policies require within a set time after the insurer asks for it."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Recoverable depreciation","description":"The depreciation withheld from an actual cash value payment that the policy allows you to collect once repairs or replacement are completed."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Replacement cost value (RCV)","description":"The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without subtracting depreciation."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Reservation of rights","description":"A letter in which the insurer continues handling a claim while reserving the right to deny coverage later."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Scope of loss","description":"The list of all damaged items and the repairs needed. Many appraisal disputes are really disputes over scope."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Square (roofing)","description":"A roofing measurement equal to 100 square feet of roof surface, used to price shingles and membranes."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Supplement","description":"A request to add items or costs to an estimate after work begins or hidden damage is found."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Tulip poplar","description":"A tall, fast-growing tree common in Maryland woodlands whose limbs and trunks often fall on homes in high winds."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Umpire","description":"The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers can’t agree on."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Waiver","description":"Giving up a right, sometimes by conduct. Delays or inconsistent actions can lead to arguments that a party waived appraisal."},{"@type":"DefinedTerm","name":"Xactimate","description":"Estimating software widely used by insurers and contractors to price repairs with regional unit costs."}],"@id":"https://marylandinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-glossary/#definedtermset"}]}</script>
</head>
