<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calculator.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="626" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Appraisal glossary</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Searchable reference</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Glossary</h1><p class="lede">40 terms from policies, estimates and appraisal awards — with Maryland-specific entries — defined in plain English.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="gfilter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “umpire”, “MIA” or “ice dam”"><p class="hint" id="gloss-count" aria-live="polite"></p></div><nav class="az" aria-label="Glossary letters"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-T">T</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></nav><dl class="glossary"><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-A">Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>The value of damaged property after depreciation is subtracted from its replacement cost. Many policies pay ACV first and the rest after repairs are completed.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Additional living expenses (ALE)</dt><dd>Extra costs of living elsewhere while a home is uninhabitable after a covered loss, such as rent and higher food costs. Usually paid under Coverage D.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The signed written decision that sets the amount of loss. Under most policies it is valid once any two of the three panel members sign it.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition that lets either party resolve a disagreement over the amount of loss through two appraisers and an umpire rather than in court.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal demand</dt><dd>The written notice one party sends the other to invoke the appraisal clause, usually naming the claim and the party’s appraiser.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-B">Betterment</dt><dd>An improvement beyond the pre-loss condition of the property. Insurers may argue that part of a repair is betterment and not owed.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-C">COMAR</dt><dd>The Code of Maryland Regulations. Title 31 contains insurance regulations, including standards for how insurers handle claims.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage A – Dwelling</dt><dd>The part of a homeowners policy that covers the house itself and attached structures such as an attached garage or deck.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage B – Other structures</dt><dd>Coverage for detached structures on the property, such as a detached garage, shed or fence, often set as a percentage of Coverage A.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage C – Personal property</dt><dd>Coverage for belongings such as furniture, clothing and electronics, whether at home or, within limits, elsewhere.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage D – Loss of use</dt><dd>Coverage for additional living expenses or lost rental income while the property can’t be used after a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-D">Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing the insured, the property, coverage limits, deductibles and endorsements.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value for age, wear and condition. It is subtracted from replacement cost to reach actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Derecho</dt><dd>A long-lived, widespread windstorm from a line of thunderstorms. The June 29, 2012 derecho caused heavy tree and roof damage across Maryland.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Disinterested appraiser</dt><dd>An appraiser with no personal or financial stake in the outcome beyond a normal fee. Most appraisal clauses require it.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-E">Examination under oath</dt><dd>A formal, recorded interview of the policyholder under oath that many policies allow the insurer to request during a claim investigation.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-H">Hurricane deductible</dt><dd>A deductible, often a percentage of Coverage A, that applies to damage from a hurricane or named storm as the policy defines it. Common near the Chesapeake Bay and the coast.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-I">Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the roof edge that traps melting snow, which can back up under shingles and into the home. A frequent winter problem in Garrett and Allegany counties.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-L">Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in a repair estimate showing a task or material, its quantity, unit and price.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-M">Maryland Insurance Administration (MIA)</dt><dd>The state agency that regulates insurers in Maryland and takes consumer complaints. It is led by the Maryland Insurance Commissioner.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Maryland Joint Insurance Association (MJIA)</dt><dd>Maryland’s insurer of last resort for property owners who can’t obtain coverage in the regular market, similar to a FAIR plan.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs match in color, texture or style, such as siding or flooring.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping a roof or drying out water. Most policies require it.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Mortgagee clause</dt><dd>The policy provision that protects the mortgage lender’s interest, which is why claim checks are often made out to both owner and lender.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-N">Named storm</dt><dd>A storm given a name by the National Hurricane Center. Some policies use this term to trigger a special percentage deductible.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Nor’easter</dt><dd>A coastal storm with strong northeast winds, heavy rain or snow and tidal flooding. The 1962 Ash Wednesday storm is a well-known Maryland example.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-O">Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs of meeting current building codes during repairs, such as upgraded electrical work or roof decking. Often limited unless increased by endorsement.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charge for running the job and earning a margin, often shown as 10% overhead and 10% profit in estimates. A frequent point of dispute.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-P">Party wall</dt><dd>A shared wall between two attached buildings, typical of Baltimore rowhouses. Fire, water and roof damage can cross party walls and complicate claims.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed that some policies require within a set time after the insurer asks for it.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-R">Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>The depreciation withheld from an actual cash value payment that the policy allows you to collect once repairs or replacement are completed.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without subtracting depreciation.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Reservation of rights</dt><dd>A letter in which the insurer continues handling a claim while reserving the right to deny coverage later.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-S">Scope of loss</dt><dd>The list of all damaged items and the repairs needed. Many appraisal disputes are really disputes over scope.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Square (roofing)</dt><dd>A roofing measurement equal to 100 square feet of roof surface, used to price shingles and membranes.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add items or costs to an estimate after work begins or hidden damage is found.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-T">Tulip poplar</dt><dd>A tall, fast-growing tree common in Maryland woodlands whose limbs and trunks often fall on homes in high winds.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers can’t agree on.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-W">Waiver</dt><dd>Giving up a right, sometimes by conduct. Delays or inconsistent actions can lead to arguments that a party waived appraisal.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt id="g-X">Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers and contractors to price repairs with regional unit costs.</dd></div></dl></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-h">Talk to an appraiser</h2><p>A free first conversation about your estimate — and a straight answer on whether appraisal fits.</p><a class="box-tel" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg>(844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn brass block" href="/start-a-claim-review/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri, 8am–6pm ET</small></div><div class="box"><h2 class="box-h">Resources</h2><ul class="box-list"><li><a href="/the-appraisal-process/">The appraisal process<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/common-questions/">Common questions<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-glossary/" aria-current="page">Appraisal glossary<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/knowledge-base/">Knowledge base<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div>
